{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_018Teogy5ddAnsi3eMSsEtV4","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-CLiksb/translated_rust/CONFIGS.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6c25b394-7046-4ff6-8cec-46c32ba03643","uuid":"c5638868-ff3f-4bf7-a6c0-183915da8eb9","timestamp":"2026-08-18T13:40:47.674Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-CLiksb/translated_rust/CONFIGS.md","content":"# CONFIGS.md — configuration surface table (valid inputs)\n\n## Build-time configuration axes\n\nEnumerated mechanically from the two build files:\n\n| source | axis | values |\n|--------|------|--------|\n| `Cargo.toml` | `[features]` | **absent** — the crate declares no features, so the only combination is the empty one |\n| `Cargo.toml` | default features | none |\n| `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` | `option()` / `if()` / `add_definitions` / `target_compile_definitions` | **none** |\n| `c_src/src/main.c` | `#if` / `#ifdef` / `#ifndef` | **none** |\n\n**Total valid feature combinations: 1** (the empty set). `cargo check\n--no-default-features` and `cargo check` are therefore the same build, and both\nare run by `scripts/verify.sh`. There is no `#[cfg(feature = ...)]` code to\ngate, because there are no features and no C preprocessor conditionals to mirror.\n\nThe one remaining build axis that *does* change generated code is the Cargo\nprofile, because `[profile.release]` sets `panic = \"abort\"`. Both profiles are\nverified (row 40).\n\n## Runtime configuration axes\n\nEnumerated from the C source. The program:\n\n- takes **no** command-line arguments (`int main()` — no `argc`/`argv`);\n- reads **no** environment variables;\n- never calls `setlocale`, so it stays in the `\"C\"` locale regardless of\n  `LC_ALL`/`LANG` (row 38);\n- has **no** flags, modes, or options of any kind — there is not a single `if`\n  or `switch` in the code.\n\nSo the entire configuration surface is (a) the **shape of the stdin byte\nstream**, (b) the **kind of descriptor** stdin/stdout are connected to, and\n(c) which **entry point** is used. The axes the code actually distinguishes,\nall inside the `scanf(\"%d\")` conversion:\n\n| axis | distinct values the C code branches on |\n|------|----------------------------------------|\n| leading whitespace | none / ` ` / `\\t` / `\\n` / `\\v` / `\\f` / `\\r` / repeated+mixed |\n| sign | none / `+` / `-` |\n| digit run | 1 digit / many / leading zeros / >19 digits / 10 000 digits |\n| magnitude class | `0` / small / `INT_MAX`±1 / `UINT_MAX`±1 / `LONG_MAX`±1 / `LONG_MIN`∓1 / far past |\n| terminating byte | EOF / whitespace / letter / punctuation / `.` / `x` / NUL / high byte |\n| conversions that succeed | 0 / 1 / 2 |\n| token count in stream | 0 / 1 / 2 / >2 |\n| stream shape | empty / whitespace-only / short / multi-MB / never reaches EOF |\n| stdin descriptor | file / pipe |\n| stdout descriptor | file / pipe / closed |\n| entry point | `main` (process) / `main` (FFI `.so`) / `driver` (FFI `.so`) |\n\n## The table\n\nEvery row is exercised with **many randomized inputs** (fixed seed `0x2545F491`,\nxorshift64\\*) unless the row is inherently a single fixed shape. `[x]` = passing\nfor both C and Rust.\n\n### `driver` — the low-level entry point, called directly through the `.so`\n\n| #  | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [x] |\n|----|----------------|-------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1  | `driver` (FFI) | full cross product of the 9-value boundary set `{0, 1, -1, 2, -2, INT_MIN, INT_MAX, 0x55555555, 0xAAAAAAAA}` for `x` × same for `y` (81 combinations) | [x] |\n| 2  | `driver` (FFI) | 4 000 uniformly randomized `(x, y)` pairs over the whole `i32` range | [x] |\n| 3  | `driver` (FFI) | correlated pairs where the result is degenerate: `y = 0` (`x\\|-1 == -1`), `y = -1` (`x\\|0 == x`), `y = x`, `y = !x`, `y = -x` | [x] |\n| 4  | `driver` (FFI) | single-bit `x` × single-bit `y` (`1<<i`, `1<<j` for all 32×32) — walks every bit position through `\\|` and `~` | [x] |\n| 5  | `driver` (FFI) | called repeatedly in one process (1 000 back-to-back calls) — checks no per-call state leaks and stdout stays byte-identical | [x] |\n\n### `main` — via the `.so` export\n\n| #  | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [x] |\n|----|----------------|-------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 6  | `main` (FFI) | stdin redirected from a temp **file**, stdout captured; two valid ints | [x] |\n\n### `main` — as a process (stdin bytes → stdout bytes + exit status)\n\n| #  | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [x] |\n|----|----------------|-------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 7  | `main` (process) | two ints, single space separator, both randomized over the full `i32` range (600 cases) | [x] |\n| 8  | `main` (process) | separator swept over every whitespace byte and repetition: ` `, `\\t`, `\\n`, `\\v`, `\\f`, `\\r`, `\\r\\n`, `\\n\\n`, and randomized mixed runs | [x] |\n| 9  | `main` (process) | leading whitespace before the first token, swept over the same whitespace alphabet and randomized runs (including runs longer than the 4 096-byte stdio buffer) | [x] |\n| 10 | `main` (process) | trailing byte after the second token: EOF immediately / `\\n` / `\\r\\n` / whitespace run / letter / punctuation | [x] |\n| 11 | `main` (process) | explicit `+` sign on the first / second / both tokens, randomized magnitudes | [x] |\n| 12 | `main` (process) | `-` sign on the first / second / both tokens, randomized magnitudes | [x] |\n| 13 | `main` (process) | leading zeros: 1–40 `0`s prefixed to a randomized value, with and without a sign (checks that base-10 does **not** switch to octal) | [x] |\n| 14 | `main` (process) | `\"0\"`, `\"-0\"`, `\"+0\"`, `\"00000\"`, `\"-00000\"` in both positions | [x] |\n| 15 | `main` (process) | `int` boundary values in both positions: `INT_MIN`, `INT_MIN+1`, `-1`, `0`, `1`, `INT_MAX-1`, `INT_MAX` (7×7 cross product) | [x] |\n| 16 | `main` (process) | just past the `int` range: `2147483648`, `-2147483649`, `4294967295`, `4294967296`, `4294967297`, `-4294967296` (cross product) | [x] |\n| 17 | `main` (process) | `long` boundaries exactly: `9223372036854775807`, `-9223372036854775808` | [x] |\n| 18 | `main` (process) | past the `long` range (`ERANGE` clamp path): `9223372036854775808`, `-9223372036854775809`, 26-digit and 40-digit magnitudes, both signs | [x] |\n| 19 | `main` (process) | randomized decimal strings of 1–25 digits with a randomized sign — sweeps across the `int`, `uint`, and `long` boundaries by construction (600 cases) | [x] |\n| 20 | `main` (process) | 10 000-digit token, with and without a sign | [x] |\n| 21 | `main` (process) | exactly **one** token in the stream, so the second conversion fails and `y` keeps `0`; randomized value, with/without trailing newline | [x] |\n| 22 | `main` (process) | **more than two** tokens (3–8), so the surplus is never read; randomized values | [x] |\n| 23 | `main` (process) | digits immediately followed by a non-digit that terminates the token: `5abc`, `5.75`, `1e5`, `0x5`, `0X5`, `5-3`, `5+3`, `12,34` | [x] |\n| 24 | `main` (process) | `0x`/`0X` forms in both positions — base-10 stops at `x`, so `0x1F` reads as `0` and leaves `x1F` | [x] |\n| 25 | `main` (process) | second token adjacent to the first with no separator (`\"12-34\"`, `\"12+34\"`) — the sign terminates token 1 and starts token 2 | [x] |\n| 26 | `main` (process) | embedded NUL bytes in every position: before, between, and inside tokens | [x] |\n| 27 | `main` (process) | high bytes `0x80`–`0xFF` (all 128 of them) as the terminating/leading byte — none is a digit or a space in the `\"C\"` locale | [x] |\n| 28 | `main` (process) | **structured fuzz**: randomized token soup drawn from {whitespace, sign, digits, letters, punctuation, NUL, high bytes} with randomized lengths (1 200 cases) | [x] |\n| 29 | `main` (process) | **raw byte fuzz**: uniformly random bytes over the full `0x00`–`0xFF` range, lengths 0–64 (1 200 cases) | [x] |\n| 30 | `main` (process) | stdin **never reaches EOF** (`yes`-style infinite producer) — C reads lazily and exits immediately; verifies the Rust build does not hang or exhaust memory | [x] |\n| 31 | `main` (process) | multi-megabyte stdin (8 MiB) whose first two tokens are valid — surplus must not be read | [x] |\n| 32 | `main` (process) | multi-megabyte stdin (8 MiB) containing **no** valid token at all | [x] |\n| 33 | `main` (process) | empty stdin (0 bytes) | [x] |\n| 34 | `main` (process) | whitespace-only stdin, short and longer than the stdio buffer | [x] |\n| 35 | `main` (process) | stdin is a **regular file** vs stdin is a **pipe** — same bytes, both descriptor kinds, for a randomized sample of the shapes above | [x] |\n| 36 | `main` (process) | stdout is a **regular file** vs stdout is a **pipe** — changes stdio buffering mode in the C build (fully buffered either way) but must not change the bytes | [x] |\n| 37 | `main` (process) | stdin closed entirely (descriptor 0 not open) | [x] |\n| 38 | `main` (process) | `LC_ALL`/`LANG` set to `C`, `POSIX`, `en_US.UTF-8`, `de_DE.UTF-8` (a locale with `,` as the decimal separator) — the program never calls `setlocale`, so output must be unchanged | [x] |\n| 39 | `main` (process) | stdin delivered in **slow drips** (one byte per write, with the writer pausing) — exercises short reads and partial buffer fills | [x] |\n| 40 | `main` (process) | Rust binary built in the **dev** profile and in the **release** profile (`panic = \"abort\"`) — both compared against the same C binary | [x] |\n\n## Coverage notes\n\n- Rows 1–5 hit the lowest-level entry point (`driver`) directly through the\n  `.so`, not just through the `main` wrapper.\n- Rows 7–39 drive the composed pipeline (`scanf` → `scanf` → `driver` →\n  `printf`/`puts`) end to end at the process boundary, which is the only place\n  the exit status, `SIGPIPE` disposition, and lazy stdin consumption are visible.\n- The error/rejection counterparts of these rows live in `ERRORS.md`.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
