{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_4lnjo4wgcq5pznlht2hvxf5gh76iyqtn4vbr7l54a7n2bycxbuxq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All 68 tests pass across every configuration.\n\n## What this project actually is\n\n`c_src/CMakeLists.txt` builds an **executable**, not a library, and the C is 14 statements with **zero branches**:\n\n```c\nvoid driver(int x, int y) { int result = x | ~y; printf(\"%d\", result); puts(\"\"); }\nint main() { int x = 0, y = 0; scanf(\"%d\", &x); scanf(\"%d\", &y); driver(x, y); return 0; }\n```\n\nSo nearly the entire behavioural surface is inherited from glibc's `scanf(\"%d\")` and from the process defaults a C program starts with. I tested at both boundaries: the process boundary (identical stdin → identical stdout/stderr/exit code/signal) and the FFI boundary (`main.c` compiled `-shared -fPIC` vs. a Rust `cdylib`, both `dlopen`ed via `libloading`).\n\n## Two real defects found and fixed\n\n**1. stdin was consumed eagerly.** The translation opened with `read_to_end`. `scanf` is lazy. With `yes 5 |` or `cat /dev/zero |`, C exits instantly while Rust burned 3–4 s allocating gigabytes, only terminating because `read_to_end` eventually returned `OutOfMemory`. On a memory-capped host it would have been OOM-killed — a different exit status. Fixed by putting the scanner directly on `BufRead::fill_buf`/`consume`, which is precisely the peek-then-commit primitive `scanf` implements with `ungetc`.\n\n**2. `SIGPIPE` was left ignored.** The Rust runtime installs `SIG_IGN` before `main`; a C program starts at `SIG_DFL`. Writing to a reader-less pipe: C dies with signal 13, Rust exited **0**. Fixed with `restore_default_sigpipe()` in the binary's `main` only — deliberately *not* in the `.so`'s `main` export, since the C `main` doesn't touch signal dispositions either.\n\n## Two harness bugs that were producing false passes\n\nI caught both by mutation testing rather than by inspection:\n\n- **Stale artifacts.** `cargo test --test <name>` doesn't rebuild `example` targets, so the FFI tests `dlopen`ed a leftover `.so` and **passed against an injected `^`-instead-of-`|` bug**. A stale `target/release/driver` separately made the SIGPIPE row pass while the fix was absent. `assert_fresh` now hard-fails on any artifact older than its sources.\n- **fd-1 interleaving.** Capturing `printf` output means redirecting a process-wide descriptor; the test harness writes progress lines to it from other threads. Each capturing test now runs in a dedicated single-threaded subprocess.\n\n## Why the check marks mean something\n\n`scripts/negative_control.sh` injects 11 realistic translation bugs and requires each to be rejected. The eager-stdin mutant **survived the first run** — `cfg30` asserted wall-clock `< 5 s` and the mutant finished in 3–4 s. I replaced that with an assertion on *bytes consumed from the endless stream* (`< 1 MiB`), where lazy vs. eager differ by six orders of magnitude instead of a factor of two.\n\n## Final state\n\n| gate | result |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` — `nm -D` diff | **empty** (`driver`, `main`); 0 unresolved symbols |\n| `CONFIGS.md` — 44 rows | all pass, 0 unchecked |\n| `ERRORS.md` — 22 rows | all pass, each asserting agreement **and** the hand-derived C value |\n| feature combinations | 1 (no `[features]`, no `#if`, no CMake `option()`) — derived as a power set, not hard-coded |\n| profiles | dev + release (`panic = \"abort\"`) |\n\nOne extra finding worth noting: `cfg43` confirms the C reference behaves identically at `-O0` and `-O2`, so \"C is ground truth\" is unambiguous here and the expected values aren't pinned to one particular build. `c_src/` was never modified (original mtime and checksum intact)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1164,"cache_read_input_tokens":200487,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1